# XCVoice – Changelog



## Version 3.2

- **Announcement order changed:** aircraft type now comes right after the
  alarm word ("Traffic, glider, 3 o'clock …"), in both languages.
- **German altitude callout reworded:** "80 metres hoch." / "80 metres
  tief." (roughly "80 metres high/low") instead of "80 metres über Dir." /
  "unter Dir." (English wording was already "above."/"below." and is
  unchanged.)
- **New "Close XCVoice" button** in settings — stops the background service
  and closes the app completely, including removing it from the
  recent-apps overview (previously the service kept running there by
  design).
- **Tapping a traffic symbol on the radar** now triggers a one-time spoken
  summary of that target: aircraft type, bearing (in whichever style is
  configured), altitude, and climb rate — independent of that alarm
  level's individual announcement toggles, since this is a deliberate
  query, not an automatic alert.

## Version 3.0

 **BLE reception** added as a third connection mode alongside classic
  Bluetooth and WiFi (TCP) — for devices like BlueConnect that expose the
  FLARM data stream over a Nordic UART Service (BLE). Built-in device scan
  right in the app, no manual pairing required.

## Version 2.4

- **Android Gradle Plugin/Gradle/SDK updated:** compileSdk/targetSdk 36,
  AGP 8.13, Gradle 8.13 — a prerequisite for a possible future Play Store
  submission (required since August 31, 2026); not strictly necessary for
  GitHub distribution, but future-proofed anyway.
- **Predictive back gesture** enabled in the manifest, matching the
  existing `BackHandler`.
- **Release keystore incident resolved:** the `xcvoice-release.jks`
  believed lost was found again in OneDrive, backed up locally, and
  signing was re-verified.
- **Flagship feature: individually configurable announcement building
  blocks per alarm level.** A long press on a level card (1/2/3) opens a
  menu where alarm type, aircraft type, relative distance, relative
  vertical, climb rate, and bearing type can each be switched on or off
  independently. A short tap still just switches the whole level on or
  off, as before. Existing global settings were automatically carried
  over to all three levels the first time the app started after the
  update.
- **TTS audio channel diagnosis:** identified the cause of silence on
  single-channel devices (missing `AudioAttributes`/channel
  configuration); recommended the system-level Mono Audio setting as a
  workaround, with a code-level fix using a custom `AudioTrack` prepared
  as a possible next step.
- **Bilingual manual (DE/EN)** created as a PDF, including screenshots,
  most recently extended to cover the per-level announcement
  configuration.
- **Multilingual marketing page** (DE/EN/FR) for xcnav.de created as
  self-contained HTML files for Wix.

## Version 2.0 (rebuild)

Complete rebuild of the app in Kotlin/Jetpack Compose to replace the
original app (package `or.nevet.flarmdisplay`, internally named
"FlarmDisplay"), after the previous developer was no longer available.
New package `de.xcnav.xcvoice`.

- More modern UI in the XCNAV colour scheme (navy/black/white with an
  orange accent)
- **New radar screen:** traffic shown as colour-coded arrows
  (green/yellow/red by alarm level) around the own position, similar to
  the separate FlarmDisplay hardware product
  - Pinch-to-zoom for range
  - Long press toggles between north-up and track-up
  - Range rings with distance labels
  - Tapping a target shows its details in the info window
- **New direction callout option:** eight sectors in plain words (e.g.
  "easy right, above"), as an alternative to the classic clock-position
  bearing
- **Test button** under the speech-rate slider, for a sample announcement
  using whichever building blocks are currently enabled
- **Bug fix:** the service kept announcing traffic after the app was
  closed or after the FLARM was switched off
- **Improved repeat logic:** reduced unnecessarily frequent repeat
  announcements of the same target
- Signed release build set up with its own keystore; distribution decided
  as GitHub Releases / xcnav.de instead of (initially) Google Play
